NAME
       awk - pattern scanning and processing language

SYNOPSIS
       awk [-F fs] [-v var=value]... [-f progfile | 'program']
          [file | var=value]...

DESCRIPTION
       The POSIX awk language: patterns and actions, BEGIN and
       END, ranges, fields and records (FS as a blank, a
       single character or an extended regular expression; RS
       as a character, "" for paragraphs, or a regular
       expression), associative arrays, user functions,
       getline in all its forms, print and printf with > >>
       and | redirections, close, fflush, system, and the
       built-in functions length substr index split sub gsub
       match sprintf sin cos atan2 exp log sqrt int rand srand
       tolower toupper, plus systime. Regular expressions come
       from the C library (extended syntax). Not implemented:
       gawk's other extensions (gensub, strftime, arrays of
       arrays, @include, BEGINFILE...), and NUL bytes in
       records.

NOTES
       Long loops yield to the kernel every 65,536 iterations,
       so the system stays responsive.

SEE ALSO
       sh(1)

